data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","item":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"toolu_bdrk_01KT9pJxdE2LJUhiwhSxNfiQ","output":"  1  ---\n  2  name: report-builder-v2\n  3  description: Builds an interactive, filterable HTML report (PowerBI-style) from a compact JSON ReportConfig via the build_report tool. Use when the user asks for a report, a báo cáo, an executive summary as a document, a dashboard-style HTML deliverable, or a shareable link to analysis results.\n  4  ---\n  5  \n  6  # Report Builder v2\n  7  \n  8  You write a **JSON `ReportConfig`**; `build_report` renders it into a self-contained\n  9  interactive report (sidebar, slicers, cross-filtering charts, RAG, sortable tables,\n 10  light/dark themes). The template owns all HTML/CSS/JS and every color — you never write\n 11  markup and never choose a theme. Your job is content, data and relationships.\n 12  \n 13  ## Workflow\n 14  \n 15  1. **Open a preset in `assets/examples/` and read it fully** — it is your template.\n 16     - `performance.json` — segment P&L. Two facts by reference out of one evidence, split\n 17       by `where`. One slicer, `insight` · `subtitle` · `kpi_group` · `driver_row` · `chart` ·\n 18       `chart_pair` · `table` (fact-driven) · `actions`, RAG. Start here.\n 19     - `campaign.json` — marketing, and the one config holding **both** fact shapes: Genie's\n 20       channel table by reference, a hand-composed monthly table inline. Adds `divider`, an\n 21       `afb_line` with `forecast_band`, and a `table` in explicit `rows` mode.\n 22     - `segment_scenario.json` — a long fact (`scenario` column: Actual/Budget/SPLY) with a\n 23       declaration-only dimension. Copy this one whenever your data has a scenario or\n 24       metric column rather than one column per scenario.\n 25  2. **Point `data.facts` at the evidence the sub-agent saved** — `{\"from\": \"wi_…\"}`, not the\n 26     rows. See *Data*.\n 27  3. **Work out what the report says with `compute_measures`, not in your head** — see\n 28     *Getting the numbers*.\n 29  4. **Declare a measure for every figure you will show**, then bind them with `{{measure}}`.\n 30  5. **Write the config with `write_file` to `/report_config.json`, then call\n 31     `build_report(config_path=\"/report_config.json\", filename=…)`.**\n 32  \n 33  Adapt the preset and send it. `build_report` validates before rendering and returns\n 34  **every** problem it found in one message, each with its location — so submitting a config\n 35  is how you check it. Re-reading your own JSON to be certain first costs more than the call\n 36  does, and finds less.\n 37  \n 38  On an `ERROR:` return, patch with `edit_file` on the same file (not a full rewrite) and\n 39  call `build_report` again with the same `config_path`. Read the errors literally and group\n 40  them: `where clause points at an undeclared dimension: 'scenario'` repeated across 13\n 41  measures is one missing declaration in `data.dimensions` — *undeclared* means **add** the\n 42  declaration, never delete the reference. `edit_file`'s `old_string` must match the file as\n 43  it stands right now.\n 44  \n 45  On success you get a link; the HTML and its source config JSON are saved side by side.\n 46  \n 47  ## Config shape\n 48  \n 49  The whole frame, and a config that builds. Every key below is spelled the way the schema\n 50  spells it; there is no other spelling and no other nesting.\n 51  \n 52  ```json config\n 53  { \"meta\": { \"label\": \"TECHCOMBANK · CIBG\", \"title\": \"CIBG — TOI 6T2026\",\n 54              \"meta_line\": \"6T2026 · nguồn: Genie · BF Hub\" },\n 55    \"data\": {\n 56      \"dimensions\": { \"sector\": { \"label\": \"Sector\", \"keys\": [\"re\", \"infra\"] } },\n 57      \"facts\": { \"sector_toi\": { \"from\": \"wi_cibg_a4c8c2c1\",\n 58                                 \"where\": { \"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\" } } } },\n 59    \"measures\": { \"toi_act\": { \"agg\": \"sum\", \"fact\": \"sector_toi\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n 60                               \"format\": \"ty1\" } },\n 61    \"filters\": [ { \"dim\": \"sector\" } ],\n 62    \"sections\": [ { \"id\": \"overview\", \"label\": \"01 — Tổng quan\", \"title\": \"Tổng quan CIBG\",\n 63                    \"blocks\": [ { \"type\": \"insight\",\n 64                                  \"html\": \"TOI đạt <strong>{{toi_act|ty1}} tỷ</strong>.\" } ] } ] }\n 65  ```\n 66  \n 67  `meta`, `data`, `sections` are required; `measures`, `filters`, `conditional_formats` are\n 68  optional. **`meta` takes exactly these keys** — anything else is rejected: `title` (the H1,\n 69  required) · `label` (eyebrow above it) · `doc_title` (browser tab) · `meta_line` · `lang` ·\n 70  `brand: {logo_url, name, footer}` · `footer: {primary, secondary}`. Period, source and author\n 71  all belong inside `meta_line`; none of them has a key of its own.\n 72  \n 73  A **section** is `{id, title, blocks[]}` (plus optional `label`, `in_nav`, `nav_level`), and\n 74  its content lives in `blocks` — not `widgets`, not `components`, not `content`. Full\n 75  contract: `contracts/report-config.schema.json`. Semantics:\n 76  `.specify/specs/001-report-builder-v2/data-model.md`.\n 77  \n 78  ## Data\n 79  \n 80  `data.facts` maps `fact_id → table`, and a table has **two shapes — the shape is where the\n 81  numbers came from**:\n 82  \n 83  | Shape | Write it as | When |\n 84  |---|---|---|\n 85  | **by reference** (default) | `{ \"from\": \"wi_…\", \"where\": {…}?, \"columns\": […]? }` | the numbers came from Genie — i.e. nearly always |\n 86  | inline rows | `[ {col: value, …}, … ]` | only a table you composed yourself: an action plan, a footnote |\n 87  \n 88  By reference, the engine opens the evidence and takes the rows itself. Nothing is retyped,\n 89  so nothing can be mistyped — and a fact you point at costs a line of config instead of two\n 90  hundred. `where` keeps matching rows (values compare exactly as printed in the file), and\n 91  two facts may share one `from` with different `where`: that is how you split actual from\n 92  budget out of one `UNION ALL` answer. `columns` keeps only the columns you name, in order.\n 93  \n 94  ```json data\n 95  \"dimensions\": {\n 96    \"sector\":   { \"label\": \"Sector\", \"keys\": [\n 97        { \"key\": \"re\", \"label\": \"Real Estate\" }, { \"key\": \"infra\", \"label\": \"Infrastructure\" } ] },\n 98    \"scenario\": { \"label\": \"Kịch bản\", \"keys\": [\"Actual\", \"Budget\"] }\n 99  },\n100  \"facts\": {\n101    \"sector_pnl\":  { \"from\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\",\n102                     \"where\": { \"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\" },\n103                     \"columns\": [\"sector\", \"scenario\", \"toi\", \"nii\", \"nfi\"] },\n104    \"action_plan\": [ { \"owner\": \"RBG\", \"item\": \"Đẩy CASA quý 3\" } ]\n105  }\n106  ```\n107  \n108  **A column you slice on must be declared in `data.dimensions`** — named by a measure `where`,\n109  by `filters[]`, or as a table `dim`. For a fact by reference the engine will declare it for\n110  you off the resolved rows, but only for a column holding **text in every row** (and fewer than\n111  50 members). One number anywhere makes the column measurable rather than sliceable, so a key\n112  column of period codes or years is always yours. Declare one yourself, too, whenever you want\n113  to fix the label or the slicer order: your members are kept as written and any the data also\n114  holds are appended.\n115  \n116  A short key list is only a risk on a column the engine cannot speak for. **Text** members are\n117  merged into what you wrote — declare `[\"re\", \"infra\"]` against data that also holds\n118  `general id` and the third is appended for you, silently and correctly. A column holding\n119  numbers anywhere is not merged, so its list is yours alone and a period the data holds and\n120  your `keys` miss fails the build naming that value. Check a declared *period* list against\n121  the data; a declared text list needs no checking. (The one text case that does bite: over 50\n122  members, where the column is passed over entirely and every value beyond your list is an\n123  error — the warning says so.)\n124  \n125  Declaring a dimension does not create a slicer, only `filters[]` does. Above, `sector` goes in\n126  `filters[]`; `scenario` stays declared and unexposed, which is what a query-only column wants.\n127  What each of the four sites actually does — and what a slicer reaches — is *One column, four\n128  places*, under **Filters**.\n129  \n130  ### Write every value the way the evidence prints it\n131  \n132  Dimension keys, both kinds of `where`, `chart_afb.months` — all of them are compared against\n133  the column as it appears in the file. The evidence header names the columns and the preview\n134  shows the values; copy from there rather than tidying on the way in.\n135  \n136  One asymmetry is worth knowing, because it is the only place the same period has two\n137  spellings. A column of whole numbers reaches the dimension layer as a *number*, so a declared\n138  key and a `where` against it — on a measure or on a chart series — carry no trailing `.0`,\n139  written either as a number or as a string. A fact `where` filters the raw file before anything\n140  is declared, so it keeps whatever the file prints.\n141  \n142  ## Getting the numbers\n143  \n144  Two kinds of figure, two routes, and there is no third:\n145  \n146  | You need it to… | Route |\n147  |---|---|\n148  | **decide what to say** — is the gap material, which sector is worst, does the trend hold | `compute_measures` |\n149  | **put it on the page** | declare a measure; the report evaluates it at render time |\n150  \n151  Both speak one language, and mostly the same words. An `expr` and a `const` are written\n152  **identically** in both. An aggregate differs in one key: the spec names the evidence\n153  directly (`\"source\": \"wi_…\"`), the config names a fact and the fact names the evidence\n154  (`\"fact\": \"sector_toi\"` → `data.facts.sector_toi.from`). So the spec you reason with becomes\n155  the config you build with. Never work a figure out twice.\n156  \n157  **Read before you compute.** Genie does not hand you raw rows; it hands you the result of\n158  SQL that already did the work. A table carrying a `vs_…` column (`vs_budget_pct`,\n159  `vs_sply_abs`) **already holds that comparison** — read the column. Deriving it again spends\n160  a round-trip to produce a second\n161  answer to a question the data has answered, and a second answer is something that can\n162  disagree with the first.\n163  \n164  You also do not need to open a table to see what is in it. The evidence block says so:\n165  `<evidence id=\"wi_…\" rows=\"164\" cols=\"section,sector,rptmonth,toi_bn_vnd,…\">` lists every\n166  column, with a preview underneath.\n167  \n168  **One call, not ten.** Write the whole set with `write_file`, then call once:\n169  \n170  ```json compute_spec\n171  {\"measures\": {\n172    \"toi_act\":  {\"agg\": \"sum\", \"source\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n173                 \"where\": {\"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\"}, \"format\": \"ty1\"},\n174    \"toi_bud\":  {\"agg\": \"sum\", \"source\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n175                 \"where\": {\"section\": \"sector_budget_2026\"}, \"format\": \"ty1\"},\n176    \"toi_ach\":  {\"expr\": \"toi_act / toi_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\"},\n177    \"headcount\": {\"const\": 10973, \"from\": \"wi_hr_2f03\"}}}\n178  ```\n179  \n180  then `compute_measures(spec_path=\"/measures.json\")`. Every call is a round-trip; ten calls\n181  is ten of them.\n182  \n183  **Say which rows you mean.** A Genie answer is often a `UNION ALL` — one table holding\n184  actual, YTD, budget, forecast and per-product rows, told apart by a `section` or\n185  `query_section` column, and usually with a `sector = \"all\"` rollup among them. An `agg`\n186  with no `where` on that table adds budget to actual to forecast and counts the rollup\n187  twice. The result comes back with the sections and their row counts attached, so a warning\n188  in the output means read it, not ignore it.\n189  \n190  **Never copy a returned value into a file.** Not into the config, not into a draft, not into\n191  a todo. If you need it again, call `compute_measures` again with the same `spec_path` — one\n192  deterministic call. A figure retyped by hand is a figure that can be retyped wrong, and that\n193  is the one failure mode nothing downstream can catch.\n194  \n195  ## Measures\n196  \n197  Every displayed number comes from a measure — exactly **one** kind per measure, since the\n198  schema branches are `additionalProperties:false` and a mixed measure fails all three as a\n199  cryptic shape error.\n200  \n201  | Kind | Shape | Reacts to filter? |\n202  |------|-------|-------------------|\n203  | aggregate | `{ \"agg\": \"sum\\|avg\\|min\\|max\\|count\\|last\\|first\", \"fact\": \"…\", \"col\": \"…\", \"where\": {…}? }` | yes — recomputed over active rows |\n204  | expr | `{ \"expr\": \"toi_act / toi_bud * 100\" }` (arithmetic over other measures only) | yes |\n205  | const | `{ \"const\": 10973, \"from\": \"wi_hr_2f03\" }` — a scalar a sub-agent returned, plus the evidence it came from | no |\n206  \n207  `where` pins an aggregate to fixed rows regardless of slicer state:\n208  `{\"scenario\": \"Actual\"}`, `{\"sector\": [\"re\",\"infra\"]}`. Its columns and values must be\n209  declared dimensions and declared keys. A fact's `where` shares the JSON shape and does a\n210  different job — it filters the file before anything is declared (see *Data*).\n211  \n212  `\"format\"` sets display only — `ty0 ty1 ty2` (10,973 / 4,349.8), `pct1` (94.8%),\n213  `signed_ty1` (+779.7 / −288), `usd_mn`, `kh`. Formatting never rescales.\n214  \n215  Number format is **intl**: `,` groups thousands, `.` is the decimal point. That is the\n216  format the data arrives in — a Genie table reads `4,349.8` — and the report prints it\n217  back unchanged. Do not re-punctuate a figure on its way into the config. `ty` in\n218  `ty0/ty1/ty2` is the unit *tỷ VND*, not a locale.\n219  \n220  ### Every figure is a token (Constitution I — inviolable)\n221  \n222  Numbers reach the page only through `{{measure}}`. `kpi_card.value` and `driver_row` box\n223  `val` must contain a token; `insight.html`, `subtitle.text`, kpi `sub` and `delta.text` are\n224  scanned for formatted numbers (`9,972.6`, `+1.6%`, `8.5`) sitting outside one. A\n225  headline that isn't summable from fact rows is a `const` measure — still bound by a token.\n226  \n227  `const` is the last door a hand-typed number could reach the page through, so it takes\n228  exactly one kind of value: a scalar a sub-agent or `compute_measures` returned, and you can\n229  name the evidence it came from. Not a figure you worked out yourself, and not one you\n230  rounded on the way in. If it can be aggregated from the facts, it is an `agg`, not a `const`.\n231  \n232  ```text\n233  WRONG — the numbers are typed:  \"sub\": \"Đạt: 101.6% · +9.2% vs Budget\"\n234  ```\n235  \n236  RIGHT — declare them in `measures`, then reference them:\n237  \n238  ```json measures\n239  { \"toi_ach_total\":  { \"expr\": \"toi_act / toi_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\" },\n240    \"nii_vs_bud_pct\": { \"expr\": \"(nii_act - nii_bud) / nii_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\" } }\n241  ```\n242  ```text\n243  \"sub\": \"Đạt: {{toi_ach_total|pct1}} · {{nii_vs_bud_pct|pct1}} vs Budget\"\n244  ```\n245  \n246  Never invent a literal, never rescale (`/1000`, `*100`), never modify sub-agent SQL or data.\n247  This is why one figure cannot appear two ways in one report.\n248  \n249  ## Blocks\n250  \n251  Every block is an object in `sections[].blocks[]`, told apart by `type`. Nine types, with\n252  the keys each one requires — `type` plus:\n253  \n254  | `type` | requires | also takes |\n255  |---|---|---|\n256  | `insight` | `html` | — |\n257  | `subtitle` | `text` | `margin_top` |\n258  | `kpi_group` | `cards[]` — each `{label, value}` | `columns` ∈ {2,3}; card `unit` `sub` `cf` `delta` `progress` |\n259  | `driver_row` | `boxes[]` (≤4) | box `delta` |\n260  | `chart` | `id`, `spec` | `title`, `sub`, `size` ∈ {sm,md,lg} |\n261  | `chart_pair` | `charts[]` — exactly 2 | — |\n262  | `table` | `columns[]` | `fact` **or** `rows[]`, `dim`, `variant`, `total`, `sortable` |\n263  | `actions` | `cards[]` — each `{priority, title, body[]}` | card `action_id` (the builder stamps one from the title — write it only to keep an action's identity when you reword its title) |\n264  | `divider` | — | — |\n265  \n266  `{{measure}}` or `{{measure|format}}` works anywhere text is shown; `<strong>`/`<em>`\n267  survive in prose, everything else is escaped.\n268  \n269  **`table`** is always click-to-sort and filter-aware. Pick exactly one row mode:\n270  fact-driven (`fact` + `columns:[{col,label,align,format,cf}]`, rows auto-generate — prefer\n271  this for detail tables) or explicit (`rows:[{key,cells}]`). Add `dim` to make rows hide\n272  under a slicer, and `total:{cells:[…]}` with tokens for a footer that recomputes.\n273  \n274  **Charts always bind a fact** — there is no inline `values`/`categories` mode, because\n275  click-to-filter needs a real column to key the slicer on.\n276  \n277  - `bar_group` / `line` — `x: {from_fact, col}` + `series: [{name, from: {fact, col, agg?, where?}}]`.\n278  - `pie` — `slices: {from_fact, name_col, value_col}`.\n279  - `afb_line` — three lines over one time axis, and **its shape differs from the two above**: a\n280    top-level `months` plus three sibling series, all three required. Each series is **looked up\n281    by period value** — for every entry of `months` the engine takes the row whose `month_col`\n282    prints the same. Row order is irrelevant, nothing is padded, and a period matching no row is\n283    a gap in that line, which is how Actual stops mid-year and Forecast starts where it stopped.\n284  \n285  **A series narrows its own fact with `where`**, the same clause a measure takes and against the\n286  same declared keys. So an evidence answering *\"TOI by sector by month by scenario\"* is **one**\n287  fact, and lines that differ only by scenario are `where` clauses over it — not one fact each.\n288  A pinned dimension stops following the slicer and whatever the `where` leaves alone keeps\n289  following it — so a line pinned to one scenario holds that scenario whatever the reader\n290  selects, and still narrows when they click a sector. Both halves in *One column, four places*.\n291  \n292  ```json chart_afb\n293  { \"kind\": \"afb_line\",\n294    \"months\": [\"202601\", \"202602\", \"202603\", \"202604\", \"202605\", \"202606\",\n295               \"202607\", \"202608\", \"202609\", \"202610\", \"202611\", \"202612\"],\n296    \"actual\":   { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n297                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"act\" } } },\n298    \"forecast\": { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n299                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"fc\" } } },\n300    \"budget\":   { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n301                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"bud\" } } },\n302    \"forecast_band\": [\"202607\", \"202612\"] }\n303  ```\n304  \n305  `months` is also the tick label, so binding a period column by reference puts period codes on\n306  the axis — the price of not retyping the numbers, and the right one. `month_col` defaults to\n307  `month`, which fits only a wide fact that already has one row per month and a column of that\n308  name (`campaign.json`).\n309  \n310  Shape limits the schema enforces: `kpi_group.columns` ∈ {2,3} (no 4-column CSS — split into\n311  a second group) · `chart_pair` holds exactly 2 charts · `driver_row` ≤ 4 boxes · `actions`\n312  priority ∈ {high, medium} · `progress` takes `width` **or** `ratio` · ids match\n313  `^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$`.\n314  \n315  ## Filters & conditional formats\n316  \n317  `filters: [ { \"dim\": \"sector\" } ]` builds the slicer and turns on chart cross-filtering.\n318  **A chart axis or pie slice bound to a declared dimension needs that dimension in\n319  `filters[]`** — without slicer state to toggle, the click would silently do nothing, so the\n320  lint rejects it. (If the axis isn't meant to be interactive, don't bind it to a dimension.)\n321  \n322  `conditional_formats` maps values to RAG classes, referenced by name from a KPI `cf`,\n323  progress `rule`, or cell `cf`:\n324  \n325  ```json conditional_format\n326  { \"thresholds\": [ {\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":85,\"class\":\"bad\"}, {\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":100,\"class\":\"warn\"} ],\n327    \"else_class\": \"good\" }\n328  ```\n329  \n330  `op: \"between\"` also needs `value2`.\n331  \n332  ### One column, four places\n333  \n334  `where`, `dimensions`, `filters[]` and a chart axis all touch the same column and do four\n335  different jobs. Nearly every question that *looks* like a report-design choice is answered\n336  here, and answered the same way every time:\n337  \n338  | The column appears in… | Runs | Does | Does **not** do |\n339  |---|---|---|---|\n340  | a fact `where` | before anything is declared, on the raw file | drops non-matching rows for good — nothing downstream sees them | make the column a dimension; the rows are gone, not pinned |\n341  | a measure or series `where` | at render, against declared keys | **pins that one dimension for that one binding** — the slicer for it stops applying *here* | pin the other dimensions; those still follow the slicer |\n342  | `data.dimensions` | build | fixes label and slicer order, and lets a `where` name the column | build a slicer |\n343  | `filters[]` | build | builds the slicer and turns on chart cross-filtering | reach a fact whose rows lack that column |\n344  | a chart `x.col` / pie `name_col` | build | the axis, and the click target | survive as a *declared* dimension that `filters[]` omits — that combination fails the build. An axis on a plain column is fine and simply does not click |\n345  \n346  Two consequences are worth reading rather than deriving, because both are cheap to state and\n347  expensive to prove from the outside:\n348  \n349  **A pinned series does not go blank.** A series carrying `where: {\"sector\": \"re\"}` still shows\n350  Real Estate when the reader selects Infrastructure: for `sector`, the pin wins. So one section\n351  can hold six sector-pinned charts *and* a live `sector` slicer — the charts stay put while\n352  every unpinned binding moves. You never need a fact pre-filtered per member, or a dropped\n353  column, to get that.\n354  \n355  **A column you never mention is never a dimension.** Inference reaches only columns the config\n356  already declares or names — in `filters[]`, a measure `where` key, or a table `dim`. A\n357  `product` column sitting in a fact nobody queries by product stays an ordinary column: no keys,\n358  nothing validated against, and no way to trip the chart lint. Adding a dimension never adds a\n359  failure, so a column you are not slicing on needs no thought at all.\n360  \n361  ### Cross-filtering across breakdowns\n362  \n363  A slicer only reaches a fact whose rows carry that dimension's column, so two breakdowns\n364  co-filter only through a shared column. In order of preference:\n365  \n366  1. **One fact at the intersection grain.** Ask Genie for the metric broken down by *both*\n367     dims in a single query and emit one fact. Every measure aggregates from it, both slicers\n368     cross-filter everything, and totals reconcile.\n369  2. **Stamp the parent key** when grains differ but a hierarchy exists (product → segment):\n370     add the parent key to each child row, and picking a `sector` narrows the product fact.\n371     The reverse stays undone on purpose — a segment total can't be re-derived from one\n372     product without product-grain data.\n373  3. **Keep them independent and say so.** Two disjoint facts are two honest lenses. Note it\n374     rather than invent an intersection Genie never returned.\n375  \n376  Model at the grain you want filtered and ask Genie for that grain. Never synthesize the\n377  intersection in the config.\n378  \n379  ## Sections\n380  \n381  A section's `label` is both the eyebrow above its H2 and its sidebar link — one source, no\n382  `nav` field. `in_nav:false` hides a section; `nav_level:1` nests it.","duration_ms":0}}

